ence of specific exam findings or lab evidence pointing toward immune activation or organ-specific inflammation", "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): ranked lower because while hyperglycemia is severe, the presence of rash and diarrhea is less typical and may suggest an overlapping condition"], "alarm_features": ["Painful rash involving multiple body regions", "Severe hyperglycemia > 500 mg/dL", "Constitutional symptoms (fatigue, malaise)", "Gastrointestinal symptoms (abdominal pain, diarrhea)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Endocrinopathy with Hyperglycemia and Rash", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.4762998362949227, "posterior": 0.5182552253514143, "danger": 0.25, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Severe hyperglycemia is not typically a primary feature of infectious or inflammatory dermatoses. Instead, it points more toward an endocrinopathy or a paraneo…"], "evidence_against": ["The vignette does not provide evidence to strongly suggest that the rash and systemic symptoms are due to an infectious or inflammatory dermatosis. This makes …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Autoimmune / Vasculitic Syndrome with Multisystem Involvement", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.048, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of specific immune activation findings in the vignette, such as elevated inflammatory markers or purpura, weakens the plausibility of an autoimmune or…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Infectious / Inflammatory Dermatosis with Systemic Features", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.061, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute multisystem involvement (rash, hyperglycemia, and gastrointestinal symptoms) moderately supports an endocrinopathy and is unlikely to be due to a …"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Gastrointestinal-Driven Systemic Syndrome", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.034, "danger": 0.15, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Neoplastic / Paraneoplastic Syndrome", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.006, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of specific evidence for glucagonoma (e.g., high glucagon levels or characteristic skin findings like necrolytic migratory erythema) and the LR+ of 0.…"]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Diabetes Mellitus with Severe Hyperglycemia and Eruptive Xanthomas", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.36710410312711, "posterior": 0.4430001257989577, "danger": 0.15, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The combination of severe hyperglycemia and obesity supports diabetes mellitus as a likely diagnosis. The presence of multisystem symptoms and a rash moderatel…"], "evidence_against": ["The painful rash is atypical for eruptive xanthomas, which are typically non-painful. This suggests that while diabetes mellitus is plausible, eruptive xanthom…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Insulin Resistance Syndrome / Metabolic Syndrome with Dermatologic Manifestation", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.09282198188583014, "posterior": 0.06475084348193112, "danger": 0.1, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Obesity and systemic symptoms support insulin resistance as a potential mechanism. The absence of classic diabetes symptoms weakens this support slightly, but …"], "evidence_against": ["A painful rash and acute worsening are not typical of insulin resistance syndrome, which is more likely to be chronic and present with acanthosis nigricans. Th…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.15, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Pancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma) with Dermatologic and Gastrointestinal Symptoms", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.0039974870305385345, "posterior": 0.001870810272934626, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["The combination of severe hyperglycemia and obesity supports diabetes mellitus as a likely diagnosis. The presence of multisystem symptoms and a rash moderatel…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.35, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Reactive Hyperglycemia Due to Infection or Inflammatory Process with Overlapping Dermatologic Symptoms", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.009, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the presence of a painful rash and severe hyperglycemia inconsistent with insulin resistance syndrome, which typically presents with non-painful dermatological changes like acanthosis nigricans?", "summary": "The painful rash is less typical of insulin resistance syndrome, which is more likely to present with acanthosis nigricans and non-painful skin changes."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of a painful rash, abdominal symptoms, and severe hyperglycemia argue against glucagonoma as the underlying condition, given the absence of characteristic skin features like migr…", "summary": "The absence of migratory erythema or other glucagonoma-specific features strongly argues against this diagnosis, especially in the face of multisystem symptoms."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of severe hyperglycemia, obesity, and multisystem symptoms (rash, fatigue, diarrhea) support the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus with eruptive xanthomas?", "summary": "The combination of severe hyperglycemia and obesity supports diabetes mellitus as a likely diagnosis. The presence of multisystem symptoms and a rash moderately supports the possibility of eruptive xanthomas."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's obesity, systemic symptoms (malaise, fatigue), and the absence of classical diabetes symptoms support insulin resistance syndrome as a potential underlying mechanism?", "summary": "Obesity and systemic symptoms support insulin resistance as a potential mechanism. The absence of classic diabetes symptoms weakens this support slightly, but it is still plausible."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a painful rash and systemic symptoms like fatigue and diarrhea argue against eruptive xanthomas as the primary dermatologic manifestation of diabetes mellitus?", "summary": "The painful rash is atypical for eruptive xanthomas, which are typically non-painful. This suggests that while diabetes mellitus is plausible, eruptive xanthomas may not explain the rash in this case."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the presence of a painful rash and worsening symptoms over the past week consistent with insulin resistance syndrome as the underlying pathophysiology?", "summary": "A painful rash and acute worsening are not typical of insulin resistance syndrome, which is more likely to be chronic and present with acanthosis nigricans. This makes insulin resistance less consistent with the observed clinical pattern."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.371], ["B1.1", 0.208], ["B1.2", 0.111]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.476], ["B1.1", 0.367], ["B1.2", 0.093]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.518], ["B1.1", 0.443], ["B1.2", 0.065]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch has posterior >= 0.75, and multiple child branches (B1.1, B1.2, B1.3) remain unresolved with non-trivial diagnostic value. Further evidence is needed to refine the differential, particularly for B1's sub-branches."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.62, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4430001257989577, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Hypertriglyceridemia', 'Insulin insensitivity', 'glucagonoma', 'Insulin resistance', 'Glucagonoma', 'Inflammatory abnormality of the skin'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_ne